#---
# purpose: "a variadic C call carries three integer arguments, in order, on every door"
#---
#
# # variadic_three_integers
#
# A C variadic call is not a call with more arguments. The platform ABI
# describes where the variadic tail goes separately from the fixed prefix, and
# on Apple's AArch64 the two places are different: fixed arguments use the
# argument registers, while every variadic argument is read off the stack.
#
# Cranelift cannot mark a call variadic, so the placement has to be produced by
# choosing the parameter list, and the call is right only if that choice is
# right for the target (see `native_codegen/variadic.rs`).
#
# Three variadic arguments are what makes the layout observable. One argument
# lands in the first slot whether the call filled the argument registers or
# not, so a single value cannot tell a correct layout from one that put the
# tail in registers. Three values in a row read back in order can.
#
# The second call goes through the same declaration with a different variadic
# arity, which is what a per-call-site parameter list buys: the shape is the
# call's, not the declaration's.
#
# `printf` reports how many characters it wrote, so the second line also pins
# the result lane: `11 22 33\n` is nine characters.
extern "C" defp libc::printf(fmt :: c_string, ...) :: c_int

def main() do
  written = libc::printf("%lld %lld %lld\n", 11, 22, 33)
  libc::printf("%lld\n", written)
end
